*   **CAA-6. Deviation Metric**: Percentile score (infra-normal <5th percentile; supra-normal >95th percentile); Z-score (supra-normal z > 1.96 for 95th percentile; infra-normal z < -1.96 for 5th percentile); Numerical encoding (+1 supra-normal, 0 normal, -1 infra-normal); Average Abnormality Index (AAI) as whole-brain summary
*   **CAA-7. Association Analysis**: Pearson correlation for AAI with polygenic risk score (PRS) and cognitive/symptom dimensions; Canonical Correlation Analysis (CCA) with permutation testing (5000 permutations) and familywise error rate control for multivariate associations between deviation scores and PRS/cognitive-symptom dimensions; Two-sample t-tests with permutation testing (5000 permutations) and False Discovery Rate (FDR) control at 5% for individual DIP items and deviation scores; Pearson correlation for AAI (CT/FA) with current IQ.
*   **CAA-8. Key Findings Brief**: Schizophrenia-related brain structural changes are highly heterogeneous across individuals, making group-consensus pathological maps unrepresentative of most individual patients, but normative modeling can help parse this heterogeneity.
*   **CAA-9. Key Findings Detailed**: Group-level analyses showed widespread reductions in FA and CT in schizophrenia patients, affecting 83% of white matter tracts and 88% of cortical regions. However, individually, no more than 17-18% of patients deviated from the normative range for any single tract or region. Despite this, 79% of patients exhibited infra-normal deviations in at least one locus (compared to 59 ± 2% of healthy individuals, p < 0.001), while 46% showed supra-normal deviations (compared to 61 ± 3% of healthy individuals, p < 0.001), indicating high individual heterogeneity in deviation loci. Higher polygenic risk for schizophrenia was associated with a greater number of regions with infra-normal CT deviations (r = -0.17, p = 0.006). Multivariate analyses revealed a significant association between PRS and deviation scores (CCA r = 0.69, p = 0.044) and a marginally significant association between symptom/cognition dimensions and deviation scores (CCA r = 0.75, p = 0.048).
*   **CAA-10. Key Limitations**: MRI data noise and artifacts; dMRI harmonization issues at smaller sites; subjective definition of normative ranges (5-95% percentiles); smaller normative sample size; individual variation in medication status, age of onset, and illness duration; potential for alternative normative modeling techniques.
*   **CAA-11. Application Notes**: Individual deviation maps can identify personalized targets for neural stimulation therapies, enable patient-centered approaches to antipsychotic drug selection, and delineate putative subtypes.

